from release_tools import package
package('V1.13.0','''# V1.13.0 — KPI Records

Enhances V1.12.0 with a KPI Records sidebar page: Retailer, Period, Sales, Quantity, Rows Count, Store %, Retailer %, Count % and filename. Each Transaction run retains a separate record, its configuration and comparison snapshot. The page supports filtering, pagination, refresh, per-store comparison details and baseline run details. Master Product inputs skip KPI evaluation. Historical Transaction runs remain visible as not captured; no KPI values are invented retrospectively.

In Client → Retailer Setup → Input Files, edit a Transaction profile to select Sales, Quantity and Store ID fields from the uploaded sample or configured rename targets. Choose the final field names after rules. Defaults are previous 8 ISO weeks, Store ±30%, Retailer ±30% and Count ±40%; weeks (1–52) and ranges (0–1000%) are configurable per input. KPI setting changes do not invalidate a parser-compatible sample. Settings are retained in configuration history and captured at batch launch, so subsequent edits do not alter an accepted run.

KPI totals use the final records after all rules. Sales and Quantity are summed, and Rows Count counts these records. The comparison uses the same retailer and Transaction input ID, one latest eligible Completed or Approved Job run per previous ISO week. Unapproved interrupted, cancelled, current-week and future-week runs are excluded. The latest usable retained output is selected if a newer eligible run cannot supply the configured fields. Historical retained outputs can supply baselines even without a KPI snapshot. Duplicate runs never multiply a week's weight. Available weekly totals are averaged; unavailable weeks are excluded rather than treated as zero, and the number and list of missing weeks are shown. Partial history is usable; no baseline requires manual review.

Change % = (current value − previous weekly average) / absolute previous weekly average × 100. Retailer % compares summed retailer sales and Count % compares final row counts. Each store compares its own sales against its average over the same eligible weeks, with zero for an absent store in an available week. Store IDs are trimmed and matched case-insensitively. Missing stores therefore show a decrease; a new store with nonzero sales against a zero baseline is flagged as percentage unavailable. Zero against zero is 0%. Store % in the main table is the mean signed store change; any individual store outside the allowed range flags the job even when this mean and retailer total are within range. Equal-to-limit changes pass. Missing/invalid mapped values make totals unavailable and require review, without publishing raw invalid values in audit messages.

Flagged runs finish as Completed with Interruption and show KPI_REVIEW_REQUIRED in Data Streams. A backend approval guard requires an explicit manual check plus a review note in KPI Records before approval. Review records the check timestamp and note in the job and batch audit. Approval is a separate action and records its timestamp; both times are displayed in Malaysia time. Existing successful jobs can still be approved, with new approval timestamps recorded. Older approval timestamps are not fabricated. Cancelled jobs retain their cancellation behavior and cannot be reviewed or approved.

Readiness warns when Transaction KPI mappings are missing. Existing input parsing, encrypted Excel, automatic reading, rules, audit, archive, comparison and store features remain included. Close the old server window and reopen the single outputs/V1.3.A1.bat launcher to run V1.13.0 at http://127.0.0.1:5001/. Local SQLite data is preserved by backup. Databases, uploads, samples, personal configuration, secrets, dependencies and generated builds are excluded from GitHub.
''','''Production Vite build and 116 backend tests passed. Ten KPI tests cover any-store flagging despite healthy aggregate/mean, exact thresholds, retailer/count deviations, one eligible run per week, input isolation, excluded statuses/future weeks, partial history, ISO year boundary, new/missing/zero-baseline stores, missing and invalid fields, finite numeric handling, Transaction-only records, historical unavailable values, captured settings, review requirements, server-side approval blocking, duplicate review/approval rejection, cancellation and review/approval timestamps. The existing 106 tests passed, including served frontend assets. Windows required explicit creation of dist/assets and a build with --emptyOutDir false. Interactive browser verification was not performed.
''',launcher=True)
